  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nScattered showers will continue southeast of a Kankakee to\nValparaiso line through midday with a few showers possible in \nthe far southeast cwa this afternoon. There may also be a few \nsprinkles or an isolated shower across the rest of the area this\nafternoon, but confidence is too low to have any mention in the\nforecast. Still some uncertainty for how fast clouds will clear\nout today, but clearing is expected this afternoon and this \nshould help high temps to reach the mid/upper 70s for most \nareas. Northeast winds over land will likely hold in the 15-25 \nmph range, but gusts over Lake Michigan may still be in the \n30-35 mph range through this evening and then winds/gusts will \nsteadily diminish tonight into Monday morning. High waves and \ndangerous swimming conditions will continue at Lake Michigan \nbeaches through Monday morning with refinement of the end time\nfor the Beach Hazard Statement possible with later forecasts.\n\nHigh pressure will allow for dry weather tonight through Tuesday\nevening. A cold front will be moving across the upper Midwest \nand western Great Lakes Tuesday night into Wednesday, possibly \nbringing decaying showers and thunderstorms to the area around \ndaybreak Wednesday morning. This front is then expected to move \nacross the area Wednesday afternoon into Wednesday night \nbringing the next chance for showers and thunderstorms. Models \nshow precipitable water values reaching 2 inches along/ahead of \nthis front and forecast qpf amounts are already in the 1-2 inch \nrange from some models. If this were to materialize, given the \nrecent rains and wet ground, additional heavy rain and localized\nflooding would be possible. Quite a bit of uncertainty for late\nweek into next weekend as the models struggle with a possible \nlarge upper trough moving into the northern Great Lakes and a \nlarge upper ridge possibly building in from the Plains. cms",
